## 6. System flows

### Stage 1 telemetry flow

```text
RFID reader -----------+
                       +--> Raspberry Pi --> Team switch
Temperature sensor ----+         |                 |
                                 |                 v
                          Local buzzer test   Organizer network
                                                   |
                                                   v
                                              Linux VM
                                                   |
                                             Receive + store
                                                   |
                                             Event display
                                                   |
                                         Screenshots/logs -> SVN
```

The VM acknowledgement proves receipt of a request. It is not an access-granted or unlock command.

### Later secure access flow — planned

```text
Card scan -> Pi creates authenticated access request
          -> VM verifies device/message and freshness
          -> VM checks prototype card permission
          -> Record accepted or denied decision
          -> Authenticated, short-lived, single-use response/command
          -> Pi verifies response before changing LED/lock state
```

Never allow an unsigned or replayed network message to activate the lock indicator or physical lock. If a physical lock is added, loss-of-power and emergency-egress behavior require an explicit design; no blanket lock-on-failure policy is assumed here.

### Later temperature-alert flow — planned

```text
Temperature reading -> Authenticated message -> VM verification
                    -> Valid reading: evaluate demo threshold
                    -> Invalid message: reject and log reason
                    -> Threshold exceeded: display alert
                    -> Local alarm or verified alarm command, as designed
```

A rejected reading must not replace the last valid reading or falsely show normal conditions. Display stale/missing data separately from safe temperature.

### Optional camera and LLM flow

```text
Card event -> Camera snapshot -> Link image to event ID

Verified event records -> LLM -> Labelled incident explanation
```

Snapshots do not prove identity. Optional face verification needs enrollment, an explicit error policy, and consideration of photo spoofing. The LLM only explains recorded events; it cannot grant access, override verification, or control the buzzer/lock. Core operation must continue if AI services are unavailable.

## 7. Proposed Stage 1 message format

Example temperature event — sample only:

```json
{
  "schema_version": 1,
  "device_id": "coldguard-pi-01",
  "boot_id": "demo-session-01",
  "event_id": "demo-session-01-0001",
  "sequence": 1,
  "event_type": "temperature",
  "timestamp": "2026-09-19T02:00:00Z",
  "simulated": true,
  "data": { "temperature_c": 6.2 }
}
```

Example RFID event — sample only:

```json
{
  "schema_version": 1,
  "device_id": "coldguard-pi-01",
  "boot_id": "demo-session-01",
  "event_id": "demo-session-01-0002",
  "sequence": 2,
  "event_type": "rfid_scan",
  "timestamp": "2026-09-19T02:00:05Z",
  "simulated": true,
  "data": { "card_alias": "demo-card-A" }
}
```

Use actual UTC timestamps when implemented. The receiver also records its own `received_at` time. Use aliases in presentation evidence rather than exposing raw card identifiers. Set `simulated` accurately. Device IDs, boot IDs, timestamps, and sequence numbers are data fields, not security controls by themselves.

Later authentication should bind the relevant message fields together. Replay state must account for restart behavior; accepting an arbitrary new boot ID must not reset trust automatically.

